  "state": {
    "case_id": "222",
    "case_summary": "A 56-year-old postmenopausal woman presented with a right breast mass noted for more than 1 year, recently associated with skin ulceration. The mass had gradually enlarged, was hard in texture, non-tender, and mobile. One month before presentation, she observed a 3-cm area of skin breakdown with mild exudation and bleeding over the lesion.\n\nOn examination, a 4.5 × 5.5 cm irregular, poorly defined, hard mass was palpable at the 1 o’clock position in the right breast, 2.5 cm from the nipple, with overlying ulceration and slight serosanguinous discharge. The lesion was fairly mobile without palpable axillary or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy. The left breast was normal.\n\nBreast ultrasound and MRI both classified the lesion as BI-RADS category V. Chest CT demonstrated a right breast mass suggestive of malignancy. Serum breast tumor markers were within normal limits.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. fibromatosis-like metaplastic carcinoma\nB. Nodular fasciitis was excluded by the absence of lymphoplasmacytic infiltration and a myxoid background\nC. Inflammatory myofibroblastic tumor was excluded by the absence of lymphoplasmacytic infiltration and a myxoid background\nD. Pseudoangiomatous stromal hyperplasia (PASH) was deemed unlikely because there was no slit\nE. Low\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
